From: IriSys contributors
Subject: [PATCH] libcody: build without distinct char8_t literals

GCC 12's bundled libcody assumes that a u8 string literal has element type
char. Since C++20 it has element type char8_t. A modern host compiler can
therefore fail while bootstrapping GCC when a recursive build overrides the
C++11 mode selected by libcody's configure script.

Pass -fno-char8_t in libcody's own compile options. This restores the source
semantics it was written for even when a parent make overrides CXX. It affects
only the host-side compiler bootstrap; target code and the guest runtime are
unchanged.

---
 libcody/Makefile.in | 1 +
 1 file changed, 1 insertion(+)

diff --git a/libcody/Makefile.in b/libcody/Makefile.in
index 1b7a4cc..94a25f4 100644
--- a/libcody/Makefile.in
+++ b/libcody/Makefile.in
@@ -22,6 +22,7 @@ INSTALL := $(srcdir)/build-aux/install-sh
 CXXFLAGS := @CXXFLAGS@
 CXXINC := $(filter -I%,@CXX@)
 CXXOPTS := $(CXXFLAGS) @PICFLAG@
+CXXOPTS += -fno-char8_t
 
 ifneq (@EXCEPTIONS@,yes)
 CXXOPTS += -fno-exceptions -fno-rtti
-- 
2.50.1
